explanation:
I found no single Mathlib declaration that states this result. The theorem takes an arbitrary algebra isomorphism `e : A/J ⊗[A] B ≃ₐ B'` and a ring map `σ : B →+* B'` that agrees with `s ↦ e (1 ⊗ s)`. It proves two things together: `σ` is surjective, and every element of the kernel of `σ` lies in `J.map (algebraMap A B)`.

Mathlib has the pieces, mainly in `Mathlib/RingTheory/TensorProduct/Quotient.lean`:
- `Algebra.TensorProduct.quotIdealMapEquivTensorQuot` identifies `B ⧸ J.map (algebraMap A B)` with `B ⊗[A] (A ⧸ J)`;
- `TensorProduct.mk_surjective` gives surjectivity;
- `mem_smul_top_of_tmul_eq_zero` and `smul_top_le` handle the kernel part.

The library's proof already combines several of these lemmas, and deriving the statement from any one of them would take more than two short lines of glue. So it is neither a duplicate nor a special case.
